<p><strong>FAA Remote Pilot Part 107 Exam Study Guide</strong></p>
<p>This chapter explains how aircraft loading, center of gravity, aerodynamic load factor, stalls, altitude, wind, and environmental conditions affect small unmanned aircraft system (sUAS) performance and safe operation.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-objectives"><strong>Chapter Objectives</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Explain how payloads and loading can affect aircraft controllability and performance.</li>
<li>Identify the primary fixed-wing flight controls and the aircraft motions and axes they control.</li>
<li>Distinguish dead reckoning, pilotage, and the wind-triangle concept.</li>
<li>Distinguish altitude above ground level (AGL) from mean sea level (MSL) altitude and describe practical ways to determine sUAS altitude.</li>
<li>Define empty weight, useful load, launch weight, and landing weight.</li>
<li>Calculate moment and center of gravity (CG) using weight-and-balance data.</li>
<li>Explain how CG location affects aircraft stability and controllability.</li>
<li>Explain load factor and how bank angle affects aerodynamic loads.</li>
<li>Explain stalls in terms of critical angle of attack and relate load factor to stall speed.</li>
<li>Describe how weight, altitude, temperature, wind, and launch-surface slope affect aircraft performance and endurance.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="5-1-loading-and-aircraft-control"><strong>5.1 Loading and Aircraft Control</strong></h2>
<p>Before each flight, the remote PIC must ensure that objects attached to or carried by the small unmanned aircraft are secure and do not adversely affect the aircraft’s flight characteristics or controllability. A battery, camera, sensor, or other payload can change both total weight and center of gravity.</p>
<p>A flight should begin with a control check. Flight-control surfaces should move freely and in the correct direction, and added equipment should not obstruct control movement. The pilot should know the correct CG location for the aircraft and understand how to check it before flight.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY POINT: Payload placement can matter just as much as payload weight. A secure payload can still make an aircraft unsafe if it moves the CG outside allowable limits.</p>
<h3 id="primary-fixed-wing-flight-controls"><strong>Primary Fixed-Wing Flight Controls</strong></h3>
