<p>Ailerons roll the aircraft around its longitudinal axis. The elevator controls pitch around the lateral axis. The rudder controls yaw around the vertical axis. On a typical Mode 2 RC transmitter, the right stick controls pitch and roll, while the left stick controls throttle and yaw.</p>
<p>MEMORY AID: Right stick = pitch and roll; left stick = throttle and yaw.</p>
<h2 id="5-2-determining-speed-course-and-altitude"><strong>5.2 Determining Speed, Course, and Altitude</strong></h2>
<h3 id="navigation-concepts"><strong>Navigation Concepts</strong></h3>
<p><strong>Dead reckoning: </strong>Navigation based on computations involving time, airspeed, distance, and direction. Wind direction and speed are used to determine heading and ground speed.</p>
<p><strong>Pilotage: </strong>Navigation by reference to recognizable landmarks or checkpoints such as roads, rivers, towns, mountains, or towers.</p>
<p><strong>Wind triangle: </strong>A vector relationship among aircraft heading and true airspeed, wind direction and velocity, and the resulting ground track and ground speed.</p>
<p>The important wind-triangle idea is that where an aircraft points is not necessarily where it travels over the ground. A crosswind can push the aircraft sideways, requiring the pilot or autopilot to point into the wind to maintain the desired course.</p>
<h3 id="altitude-agl-versus-msl"><strong>Altitude: AGL versus MSL</strong></h3>
<p>Part 107 operations generally keep a small UAS at or below 400 feet above ground level (AGL). AGL is the aircraft’s height above the surface below it. Mean sea level (MSL) altitude is referenced to sea level and therefore is not the same measurement.</p>
<p>If an aircraft reports 5,400 feet MSL and the terrain directly below it is 5,000 feet MSL, its approximate height is 400 feet AGL. Pilots must understand what altitude reference their particular UAS or control station displays.</p>
<ul>
<li>Use a calibrated barometric altimeter and convert MSL altitude to AGL using terrain elevation when necessary.</li>
<li>Use GPS/GNSS altitude while understanding the reference used by the system.</li>
<li>Use the control station’s displayed altitude or height when its reference is understood.</li>
<li>Use known terrain, structures, or other objects as supporting visual references.</li>
<li>A visual observer may use a measured ground distance to develop a rough visual sense of 400 feet, but this is only an approximation.</li>
</ul>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP: 400 feet above the takeoff point is not necessarily 400 feet AGL. Rising or falling terrain changes the aircraft’s actual height above the ground.</p>
<h2 id="5-3-aircraft-loading"><strong>5.3 Aircraft Loading</strong></h2>
<div class="textbook-table" role="region" aria-label="5.3 Aircraft Loading — table 2" tabindex="0"><table><caption>5.3 Aircraft Loading — table 2</caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Term</th>
<th scope="col">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Empty weight</th>
<td>The aircraft’s basic weight, including the airframe, fixed equipment, and—under the lesson definition—the power source/unusable fuel as applicable.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Useful load</th>
<td>Items added for the mission, such as usable fuel or battery and payload equipment.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Launch weight</th>
<td>Empty weight plus useful load; the aircraft’s weight at the beginning of flight.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Landing weight</th>
<td>Launch weight minus fuel consumed or any load released or jettisoned during the flight.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Increasing launch weight generally increases the lift and power required to fly. It can affect launch distance, climb performance, stall speed, maneuverability, and endurance. A battery-powered aircraft normally returns at nearly the same mass at which it launched, while a fuel-powered aircraft becomes lighter as fuel is consumed.</p>
<h2 id="5-4-computing-weight-and-balance"><strong>5.4 Computing Weight and Balance</strong></h2>
<p><strong>Datum line: </strong>A manufacturer-established reference point along the aircraft’s longitudinal axis from which arms are measured.</p>
<p><strong>Arm: </strong>The horizontal distance from the datum to the location of an item.</p>
<p><strong>Moment: </strong>The rotational effect of a weight at a distance from the datum.</p>
<p><strong>Center of gravity (CG): </strong>The aircraft’s balance point, expressed as a distance from the datum.</p>
<p><strong>Moment = Weight × Arm</strong></p>
<p><strong>CG = Total Moment ÷ Total Weight</strong></p>
<p>A CG that is too far forward generally increases longitudinal stability but may make it difficult to pitch the nose up. A CG that is too far aft reduces longitudinal stability and can make the aircraft difficult or unsafe to control. Lateral balance also matters: mounting a heavy battery or sensor off-center can create a left-right imbalance.</p>
<h3 id="worked-weight-and-balance-example"><strong>Worked Weight-and-Balance Example</strong></h3>
