<p>Because the moments in the table are expressed in thousands of pound-inches, multiply the total moment by 1,000 when computing CG:</p>
<p><strong>CG = (100.28 × 1,000) ÷ 2,407 = 41.66 inches from the datum</strong></p>
<p>The calculation alone does not establish that the loading is safe. The resulting CG must be compared with the manufacturer’s allowable CG range. The same process applies to a UAS: determine weights and arms, calculate moments, find total weight and total moment, calculate CG, and verify that the CG is within limits.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">KEY POINT: The remote PIC should use current weight-and-balance data. Changing a battery, camera, sensor, or payload can change both total weight and CG.</p>
<h2 id="5-5-load-factor"><strong>5.5 Load Factor</strong></h2>
<p>In aerodynamics, load is the force or stress the aircraft structure must support in flight. Load factor describes the aerodynamic load on the aircraft compared with its weight and is commonly expressed in G units.</p>
<p>In straight-and-level, unaccelerated flight, an aircraft experiences approximately 1 G. During maneuvering, the load factor can increase or decrease. In a level turn, part of the lift vector acts horizontally to turn the aircraft. To maintain altitude, the wings must generate additional total lift, increasing load factor.</p>
