<p>The increase is modest at shallow bank angles but becomes very rapid beyond about 45 degrees. At 60 degrees of bank, a level coordinated turn produces approximately 2 G, meaning the wings must generate roughly twice the aircraft’s weight in lift.</p>
<p><strong>More bank → more required lift → higher load factor → higher stall speed</strong></p>
<h2 id="5-6-stalls-and-angle-of-attack"><strong>5.6 Stalls and Angle of Attack</strong></h2>
<p><strong>Airfoil: </strong>A structure or body shaped to produce a useful aerodynamic reaction to airflow.</p>
<p><strong>Chord line: </strong>An imaginary straight line from the leading edge to the trailing edge of an airfoil.</p>
<p><strong>Relative wind: </strong>The airflow experienced by the airfoil, acting opposite the aircraft’s flight path.</p>
<p><strong>Angle of attack (AOA): </strong>The angle between the airfoil’s chord line and the relative wind.</p>
<p>As angle of attack increases, lift generally increases until the wing reaches its critical angle of attack. Beyond that point, airflow separates significantly from the upper surface, lift decreases sharply, and the wing stalls.</p>
<p>The lesson identifies a typical critical angle in the neighborhood of 18–20 degrees for many airfoils, while noting that the actual value depends on the airfoil and aircraft design. The essential concept is not a particular number: a stall occurs when the critical angle of attack is exceeded.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout">EXAM TIP: An aircraft does not stall merely because it is flying slowly. A wing stalls when it exceeds its critical angle of attack. It can therefore stall at any airspeed, altitude, weight, or attitude if that critical angle is exceeded.</p>
<p>Load factor and stall speed are connected. In a steep level turn, the wing must generate more lift. The higher load factor causes the aircraft to reach its critical angle of attack at a higher airspeed. Therefore, increasing load factor increases stall speed.</p>
<h2 id="5-7-aircraft-performance"><strong>5.7 Aircraft Performance</strong></h2>
<p>The remote PIC is responsible for determining whether the planned flight can be accomplished safely without creating an undue hazard or increasing the likelihood of losing positive control. Aircraft configuration, payload, launch area, and environmental conditions must all be considered before launch.</p>
<h3 id="factors-affecting-launch-and-flight-performance"><strong>Factors Affecting Launch and Flight Performance</strong></h3>
<p><strong>Weight: </strong>A heavier aircraft requires more lift and generally more power and launch speed. Increased weight can degrade climb, maneuverability, and endurance.</p>
<p><strong>Altitude: </strong>Higher altitude contributes to higher density altitude and reduced air density, which can reduce aerodynamic and propeller performance.</p>
<p><strong>Temperature: </strong>Higher temperature reduces air density and can degrade aircraft performance, especially when combined with high elevation.</p>
<p><strong>Wind: </strong>A headwind generally improves fixed-wing launch performance by reducing the ground speed needed to obtain the required airspeed. A tailwind increases required ground speed and launch distance.</p>
<p><strong>Runway or launch-surface slope: </strong>For a rolling fixed-wing takeoff, an upslope adds a retarding component and increases the distance required to accelerate; a downslope assists acceleration.</p>
<h3 id="endurance-versus-range"><strong>Endurance versus Range</strong></h3>
<p>Maximum endurance means remaining airborne for the greatest amount of time. For a fixed-wing aircraft, maximum endurance occurs near the condition of minimum power required to maintain flight. For an electric UAS, this corresponds to a flight condition requiring the least battery power necessary to remain airborne.</p>
<p>Endurance and range are different. Endurance asks, “How long can I stay airborne?” Range asks, “How far can I travel?” The airspeed that maximizes endurance is not necessarily the same airspeed that maximizes range.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-5-summary"><strong>Chapter 5 Summary</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>The remote PIC must ensure that payloads are secure and do not adversely affect flight characteristics or controllability.</li>
<li>Ailerons control roll, the elevator controls pitch, and the rudder controls yaw.</li>
<li>Dead reckoning uses calculations; pilotage uses visual landmarks; the wind triangle relates heading, airspeed, wind, track, and ground speed.</li>
<li>AGL is height above the ground below the aircraft; MSL is altitude referenced to sea level.</li>
<li>Aircraft loading affects performance, stability, and controllability.</li>
<li>Moment equals weight times arm; CG equals total moment divided by total weight.</li>
<li>A CG outside approved limits can make an aircraft difficult or impossible to control.</li>
<li>Load factor increases rapidly as bank angle becomes steep; a 60-degree level turn produces about 2 G.</li>
<li>A stall occurs when the critical angle of attack is exceeded, not simply because the aircraft is slow.</li>
<li>Higher load factor increases stall speed.</li>
<li>Weight, altitude, temperature, wind, and launch-surface slope can affect launch and flight performance.</li>
<li>Maximum endurance occurs near minimum power required, and maximum endurance is not the same as maximum range.</li>
</ul>
